"Date"] [:td {} "27 September 1988"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/655"} "Jang-chung Che-yuk-gwan, Seoul"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/16571"} "41 from 41 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two single-elimination pools, with winner of each pool advanced to the final. All judoka losing to the winner of each pool advanced to repêchage pools, with the winners of the repêchage pools earning bronze medals."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The lightweight class was wide-open. At least six fighters were felt capable of winning the gold medal. These included the reigning World Champion, American " [:a {:href "/athletes/34428"} "Mike Swain"] "; the 1987 World runner-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/33397"} "Marc Alexandre"] " (FRA), also the 1984 half-lightweight bronze medalist; " [:a {:href "/athletes/33938"} "Toshihiko Koga"] ", the All-Japan Champion; and Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/33522"} "Kerrith Brown"] ", bronze medalist at the 1987 Worlds. In the pool semis, Swain met East German " [:a {:href "/athletes/34014"} "Sven Loll"] ". Swain led early, but Loll eliminated him with a late " [:em {} "yuko"] ". Loll went to the final where he met Alexandre. Both were " [:em {} "uchimata"] " specialists, but neither could score with it. Alexandre won gold with a late " [:em {} "koka"] " from " [:em {} "kouchi-gari"] ".  In one of the bronze medal matches, Brown defeated Swain with a " [:em {} "koka"] " from a side-sacrifice throw at 3½ minutes. But the medals were not yet decided. Two days later, Brown was disqualified for doping, testing positive for furosemide (Lasix), a diuretic. The bronze medal was given to Swain, joined by Soviet Georgian " [:a {:href "/athletes/34452"} "Giorgi Tenadze"] "."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}